## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical QoS, tenant isolation, and rate-limiting design in llm-ops pipelines, focusing on observable behavior under bursty traffic.

## Key Concepts

- Token bucket rate limiting per tenant
- Burst handling and graceful degradation
- Observability: per-tenant latency, token burn, retry metrics
- SLA verification and alerting during bursts

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal token bucket for per-tenant pacing
class TokenBucket {
  constructor(ratePerSec, capacity) {
    this.rate = ratePerSec;
    this.capacity = capacity;
    this.tokens = capacity;
    this.last = Date.now();
  }
  tryAcquire() {
    const now = Date.now();
    const elapsed = (now - this.last) / 1000;
    this.last = now;
    this.tokens = Math.min(this.capacity, this.tokens + elapsed * this.rate);
    if (this.tokens >= 1) {
      this.tokens -= 1;
      return true;
    }
    return false;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test per-tenant QoS in staging and production?
- How would you handle cross-region tenants with different latency profiles?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to design a practical, testable fair-use layer for a multi-tenant LLM gateway with billing considerations.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant quotas and burst handling using a token bucket
- Isolation of prompts and responses per tenant
- Simple REST API for quota status and consumption
- Observability and a minimal test plan for bucket behavior

## Code Example
```javascript
class TokenBucket {
  constructor(capacity, refillPerMs) {
    this.capacity = capacity;
    this.tokens = capacity;
    this.refillPerMs = refillPerMs;
    this.lastRefill = Date.now();
  }
  tick() {
    const now = Date.now();
    const elapsed = now - this.lastRefill;
    const refill = Math.floor(elapsed * this.refillPerMs);
    if (refill > 0) {
      this.tokens = Math.min(this.capacity, this.tokens + refill);
      this.lastRefill = now;
    }
  }
  tryConsume(n = 1) {
    this.tick();
    if (this.tokens >= n) {
      this.tokens -= n;
      return true;
    }
    return false;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design dynamic, policy-driven routing with on-edge latency constraints, multi-tenant safeguards, and auditable provenance. It also probes risk scoring, drift detection, and rollback semantics under real-world operational pressures.

## Key Concepts

- Contextual bandits for real-time routing decisions
- Per-tenant safety profiles and data locality constraints
- Latency budgets and SLA adherence (p95 targets)
- Drift detection and automated rollback to baseline
- Tamper-evident, cryptographically signed per-tenant audit logs

## Code Example

```javascript
function selectVariant(context, stats) {
  // context: {tenant, latency, safetyProfile}
  // stats: {risk, base, tuned, safety}
  if (Math.random() < 0.1) return 'base';
  if (stats.risk > 0.6) return 'safety-guarded';
  const scores = { base: stats.base, tuned: stats.tuned, safety: stats.safety };
  return Object.keys(scores).reduce((a,b) => scores[a] > scores[b] ? a : b);
}
```

